Route uv_spawn's child half through the kernel's spawn primitive instead of
fork+exec.

The kernel performs the whole tail upstream runs in the forked child --
resetting signal dispositions, placing the stdio descriptors, redirecting the
ignored standard three to /dev/null, setsid, setgroups/setgid/setuid, chdir,
the environment, the empty signal mask, and the PATH search execvp would have
done -- so uv__spawn_and_init_child becomes a request struct and one syscall.
uv__process_child_init, uv__spawn_and_init_child_fork, the error pipe with its
uv__write_int/uv__write_errno pair and the cloexec_lock bracket all go with the
fork they existed to serve. The syscall's return value replaces the error pipe
outright: it is the child's pid, or a negative errno already shaped like
UV__ERR, and the exec has already happened by the time it returns, so there is
no window in which the parent must ask a not-yet-exec'd child how it went. The
request layout and the syscall number are declared once, in the sysroot's
visorjs-spawn.h; nothing here restates them.

The request always carries an envp, because the kernel has no second source
for one. A NULL options->env is upstream's execvpe(file, args, environ) case,
so it becomes this process's live environ here rather than a 0 the kernel
would have to reconstruct from the exec-time environ block -- a block musl
leaves behind the moment a setenv reallocs __environ onto the heap. The
kernel's PATH search reads the same vector, so a stale one resolves a
different binary silently; a 0 is EINVAL there now.

The deletion is the point, not the syscall. A fork() call site forces
frame-record instrumentation onto every function that can be an ancestor of it,
and a spawn issued from JS puts the whole JavaScript engine on that chain. A
fallback that is never taken costs exactly the same, because that reachability
is decided statically and an indirect call counts as may-reach -- so there is
no runtime selection here and no second path to fall back to.

The split is a #if rather than a delete only because deps/uv/uv.gyp builds
libuv for both toolsets: node_js2c runs on the build machine and links the host
copy, compiled by the host compiler against the build container's own include
path, which carries no visorjs-spawn.h. That branch is upstream's, unmodified,
and reaches no wasm binary -- preprocessed for this target, the file has no
fork, vfork, execvp, execve, posix_spawn, setsid, setuid or sigprocmask left in
it at all.

Only the child half moves. uv_spawn keeps its handle registration, its SIGCHLD
child_watcher, uv__process_init_stdio and uv__process_open_stream; both ends of
every socketpair it made are still open in the caller when the syscall runs,
which is what lets the kernel install the child's ends out of the caller's own
descriptor table. src/spawn_sync.cc reaches this same uv_spawn on a private
loop, so the synchronous API takes the path too.

Upstream's first of two fd passes has no counterpart here. It exists only
because dup2 rewrites a single table in place -- swapping stdout and stderr
would otherwise clobber one with the other -- and the kernel builds the child's
table from the caller's rather than mutating it, so no slot is ever both a
source and a destination.

--- a/deps/uv/src/unix/process.c
+++ b/deps/uv/src/unix/process.c
@@ -34,6 +34,10 @@
 #include <unistd.h>
 #include <fcntl.h>
 #include <poll.h>
+
+#if defined(__wasm__)
+# include <visorjs-spawn.h>
+#endif
 
 #if defined(__APPLE__)
 # include <spawn.h>
@@ -265,7 +269,77 @@
   uv__stream_close(container->data.stream);
 }
 
+
+#if defined(__wasm__)
+static int uv__spawn_and_init_child(
+    uv_loop_t* loop,
+    const uv_process_options_t* options,
+    int stdio_count,
+    int (*pipes)[2],
+    pid_t* pid) {
+  struct visorjs_spawn_req req;
+  int32_t map_storage[8];
+  int32_t* stdio_map;
+  long r;
+  int fd;
 
+  (void) loop;  /* No error pipe to bracket, so no cloexec_lock either. */
+
+  /* One entry per child fd: the caller's fd to install there, or -1.
+   * uv__process_init_stdio has already left exactly that in pipes[fd][1]. */
+  stdio_map = map_storage;
+  if (stdio_count > (int) ARRAY_SIZE(map_storage)) {
+    stdio_map = uv__malloc(stdio_count * sizeof(*stdio_map));
+    if (stdio_map == NULL)
+      return UV_ENOMEM;
+  }
+
+  for (fd = 0; fd < stdio_count; fd++)
+    stdio_map[fd] = pipes[fd][1];
+
+  memset(&req, 0, sizeof(req));
+  req.file = (uint64_t) (uintptr_t) options->file;
+  req.argv = (uint64_t) (uintptr_t) options->args;
+  /* execvpe(options->file, options->args, environ) is what upstream runs
+   * for a NULL env, so a NULL one is this process's LIVE environ. envp is
+   * required: the kernel cannot read it back after a setenv has moved it. */
+  req.envp = (uint64_t) (uintptr_t)
+      (options->env != NULL ? options->env : environ);
+  req.cwd = (uint64_t) (uintptr_t) options->cwd;
+  req.stdio_map = (uint64_t) (uintptr_t) stdio_map;
+  req.stdio_count = (uint32_t) stdio_count;
+
+  if (options->flags & UV_PROCESS_DETACHED)
+    req.flags |= VISORJS_SPAWN_SETSID;
+
+  if (options->flags & UV_PROCESS_SETGID) {
+    req.flags |= VISORJS_SPAWN_SETGID;
+    req.gid = (uint32_t) options->gid;
+  }
+
+  if (options->flags & UV_PROCESS_SETUID) {
+    req.flags |= VISORJS_SPAWN_SETUID;
+    req.uid = (uint32_t) options->uid;
+  }
+
+  /* execvp searches only a name with no slash in it. */
+  if (strchr(options->file, '/') == NULL)
+    req.flags |= VISORJS_SPAWN_SEARCH_PATH;
+
+  r = visorjs_spawn(&req, sizeof(req));
+
+  if (stdio_map != map_storage)
+    uv__free(stdio_map);
+
+  if (r < 0)
+    return (int) r;  /* Already UV__ERR-shaped: errno is not folded. */
+
+  *pid = (pid_t) r;
+  return 0;
+}
+
